• Yes. Do it as an upgrade-style test.

  Use an older/pre-change build to create CBC commitlogs, then start current HEAD with GCM using the same data/commitlog dirs and the same keystore.

  1. Make old worktree

  OLD=/tmp/cassandra-cbc-old
  NEW=/media/ext/projects/open_source/cassandra_5/cassandra
  ROOT=/tmp/cassandra-cbc-gcm-test

  git worktree add "$OLD" 2fb00a2074
  JAVA_HOME=/usr/lib/jvm/java-17-openjdk ant -f "$OLD/build.xml" jar
  JAVA_HOME=/usr/lib/jvm/java-17-openjdk ant -f "$NEW/build.xml" jar

  2fb00a2074 is before CBC was made read-only for new writes.

  2. Create shared dirs and key

  rm -rf "$ROOT"
  mkdir -p "$ROOT"/{data,commitlog,hints,saved_caches,cdc_raw,conf-cbc,conf-gcm}

  keytool -genseckey \
    -alias tde_aes_256 \
    -keyalg AES \
    -keysize 256 \
    -storetype JCEKS \
    -keystore "$ROOT/tde.jceks" \
    -storepass cassandra \
    -keypass cassandra

  3. Create two configs

  Copy old config twice:

  cp -a "$OLD/conf/." "$ROOT/conf-cbc/"
  cp -a "$NEW/conf/." "$ROOT/conf-gcm/"

  In both cassandra.yaml files set the same directories:

  hints_directory: /tmp/cassandra-cbc-gcm-test/hints
  data_file_directories:
    - /tmp/cassandra-cbc-gcm-test/data
  commitlog_directory: /tmp/cassandra-cbc-gcm-test/commitlog
  saved_caches_directory: /tmp/cassandra-cbc-gcm-test/saved_caches
  cdc_raw_directory: /tmp/cassandra-cbc-gcm-test/cdc_raw

  In conf-cbc/cassandra.yaml:

  transparent_data_encryption_options:
    enabled: true
    chunk_length_kb: 64
    cipher: AES/CBC/PKCS5Padding
    key_alias: tde_aes_256
    iv_length: 16
    key_provider:
      - class_name: org.apache.cassandra.security.JKSKeyProvider
        parameters:
          - keystore: /tmp/cassandra-cbc-gcm-test/tde.jceks
            keystore_password: cassandra
            store_type: JCEKS
            key_password: cassandra

  In conf-gcm/cassandra.yaml:

  transparent_data_encryption_options:
    enabled: true
    chunk_length_kb: 64
    cipher: AES/GCM/NoPadding
    key_alias: tde_aes_256
    iv_length: 12
    key_provider:
      - class_name: org.apache.cassandra.security.JKSKeyProvider
        parameters:
          - keystore: /tmp/cassandra-cbc-gcm-test/tde.jceks
            keystore_password: cassandra
            store_type: JCEKS
            key_password: cassandra

  4. Start old CBC Cassandra

  Terminal 1:

  CASSANDRA_CONF=/tmp/cassandra-cbc-gcm-test/conf-cbc "$OLD/bin/cassandra" -f

  Terminal 2:

  "$OLD/bin/cqlsh" -e "
  CREATE KEYSPACE cbc_test WITH replication = {'class':'SimpleStrategy','replication_factor':1};
  CREATE TABLE cbc_test.t (pk int PRIMARY KEY, v text);
  INSERT INTO cbc_test.t (pk, v) VALUES (1, 'written-by-cbc');
  "

  Then kill the old process hard, not graceful shutdown:

  pkill -9 -f "$OLD.*CassandraDaemon"

  Check CBC header:

  strings /tmp/cassandra-cbc-gcm-test/commitlog/CommitLog-*.log | grep 'AES/CBC'

  5. Start current GCM Cassandra

  CASSANDRA_CONF=/tmp/cassandra-cbc-gcm-test/conf-gcm "$NEW/bin/cassandra" -f

  Verify old CBC replayed:

  "$NEW/bin/cqlsh" -e "SELECT * FROM cbc_test.t WHERE pk = 1;"

  Verify new GCM write works:

  "$NEW/bin/cqlsh" -e "
  INSERT INTO cbc_test.t (pk, v) VALUES (2, 'written-by-gcm');
  SELECT * FROM cbc_test.t;
  "

  Check new commitlog header:

  strings /tmp/cassandra-cbc-gcm-test/commitlog/CommitLog-*.log | grep 'AES/GCM'

  Key points: generate CBC with old code, start current code with GCM, and keep the old CBC key alias available in the current key provider.